---INSTRUCTION---
Write an Elixir function that takes a list of integers as input, reverses the list, and returns the reversed list with every second element after the first removed.

The function should have the following specification:

- Function name: reverse_and_remove
- Input: a list of integers.
- Output: a list of integers.

The function should return a new list where:
1. The input list is reversed.
2. From the reversed list, take the first element and then every second element (i.e., elements at even indices in the reversed list).

Complexity: The function should run in O(n) time, where n is the length of the input list.

Edge cases:
- If the input list is empty, return an empty list.
- If the input list has one element, return a list with that element.
- If the input list has two elements, return a list with the last element of the input (which becomes the first of the reversed list).

Do not use any external libraries. The function should be pure.

---TEST---
defmodule SolutionTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  test "reverse_and_remove with [10,20,30,40,50,60]" do
    assert Solution.reverse_and_remove([10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]) == [60, 40, 20]
  end

  test "reverse_and_remove with [1,2,3,4,5]" do
    assert Solution.reverse_and_remove([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) == [5, 3, 1]
  end

  test "reverse_and_remove with [1,2]" do
    assert Solution.reverse_and_remove([1, 2]) == [2]
  end

  test "reverse_and_remove with empty list" do
    assert Solution.reverse_and_remove([]) == []
  end

  test "reverse_and_remove with single element" do
    assert Solution.reverse_and_remove([42]) == [42]
  end

  test "reverse_and_remove with two elements different order" do
    assert Solution.reverse_and_remove([100, 200]) == [200]
  end

  test "reverse_and_remove with even length [1,2,3,4]" do
    assert Solution.reverse_and_remove([1, 2, 3, 4]) == [4, 2]
  end

  test "reverse_and_remove with odd length [1,2,3,4,5,6,7]" do
    assert Solution.reverse_and_remove([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]) == [7, 5, 3, 1]
  end
end

---REFERENCE---
defmodule Solution do
  @doc """
  Reverse the input list and then take every second element starting from the first.

  ## Examples

      iex> Solution.reverse_and_remove([1, 2, 3, 4])
      [4, 2]

  """
  def reverse_and_remove(list) do
    list
    |> Enum.reverse()
    |> Enum.take_every(2)
  end
end

